{"guide":{"questionsToConsider":["Is this for one show, an ongoing open call or a publication, and does the greeting say which?","Are there limits on size, medium or year that artists should know before they start?","Will you take a commission on sales, and should the price question say so?","How many works may each artist submit, and should they fill in the form once per piece?"],"howToUseResponses":"Export the submissions to CSV and review the images alongside the medium, size and year, so the selection panel can picture each piece in the space. Pass the handling notes for sculpture and installation to whoever plans the hang early. Keep the bios and prices together for the wall labels and price list once the selection is made.","customizeSteps":["Add the deadline, theme and any size limits to the greeting.","Edit the medium list to match what your show accepts.","Share the link in your open call announcement, or embed it on the call page."],"faqs":[{"q":"What should an art submission form ask for?","a":"Images of the work, the title, medium, dimensions and year, an artist statement, contact details, and whether the work is for sale. Permission to reproduce the images is worth asking for up front."},{"q":"What image files should artists submit?","a":"High resolution JPEG or PNG files that show the whole piece clearly, plus a few detail shots. This form accepts up to five images per work."},{"q":"How long should an artist statement be for a submission?","a":"A short paragraph is usually enough: what the work is about and how it was made. Curators read many of them, so clear beats long."},{"q":"Can artists submit more than one piece?","a":"Yes. The simplest way is to fill in the form once per piece, which keeps each work's details and images together in your responses."}]},"doc":{"schemaVersion":10,"title":"Art submission","description":"Receive artwork and the context a curator or editor needs to review it.","blocks":[{"id":"blk_artsubmi00","ref":"welcome","title":"